// The `prelude.store` sub-module: the project's durable key-value store — named values that
// outlive any single run. Where a handler's `var` is state WITHIN a run and `env` is operator-set
// configuration INTO a run, the store is state BETWEEN runs: any run reads what another wrote, the
// operator browses and edits the same tree in the console, and an AI reads a stored note back into
// its context. Values are ordinary Katari values: a document, a data value, a `file`, a secret
// (`string of private` seals at rest exactly like a secret env entry, and stays redacted at every
// user-facing boundary). Writes are last-write-wins.
//
// KEYS ARE AMBIENT. An operation names a path-like key (`"memos/2026-07"`) and nothing else; WHERE
// the key lands is decided by the environment, not by a value threaded through the code. Four
// concepts cover the whole module:
//
//   - WORKSPACE (`scope`): the store's working directory, opened as a provider. Under
//     `use store.scope(path = "core")` every operation's key is prefixed with `core/`; nested
//     scopes accumulate prefixes; the outermost bare perform is project-root access. An agent
//     dispatched inside a workspace lives in that directory BY CONSTRUCTION — it holds no value
//     it could widen. A path is `/`-separated `safe_segment`s and a malformed one panics, because
//     that construction is only as sound as the strings a program puts in it.
//   - FACILITY: a workspace-relative toolset — a package (a memory, a task list) whose tools open
//     ONE fixed subdirectory scope (`use store.scope(path = "memory")`) around their own
//     operations. The path is the package's constant; WHERE the facility's data lives is the
//     CALLING workspace's decision, which is the whole inversion.
//   - SERIAL DOMAIN (`serialize` / `exclusive` / `modify`): a FIFO for read-modify-writes, installed
//     for the extent of one dispatch (a turn), so the concurrently dispatched tools of one turn
//     cannot interleave their critical sections. The OUTERMOST domain is the runtime itself: an
//     `exclusive` no program handler catches is served at the PROJECT ROOT — one durable,
//     project-wide FIFO across every run, exactly as the outermost bare `get` is project-root access.
//   - SHARED PLACE (`share` / `shared`): data several workspaces must read never travels by path
//     escape — there is deliberately no `..`. It travels as a REQUEST served ABOVE the workspaces,
//     whose clause opens the shared subtree in its own body; a workspace can never capture what it
//     does not serve, so the proxy IS the semantics of sharing. `shared` is that proxy with the CELL
//     factored out: the task is a closure, so ONE request covers every shared cell.
//
// Each operation is a `request` — part of the run's ENVIRONMENT. A program performs it; a handler in
// scope may catch it (the same mechanism that catches `prelude.throw`) to intervene — a workspace
// prefix, a test stub, a redirect. Unhandled, it escalates like any request to the run's OUTERMOST
// environment, the runtime itself, which MACHINE-ANSWERS it against the project's durable rows at
// the PROJECT ROOT — it is never surfaced as a question to a human operator. The answer is durable,
// so a re-run during recovery observes the same value the first attempt did: the store is
// replay-deterministic.
//
// Storing a `file` JOINS its bytes to the project's FILE LIBRARY: the stored file becomes a project
// file (owned by the api root, listed on the console's Files page) that outlives the run that wrote
// it. Overwriting or deleting an entry NEVER frees a file — the store only forgets the reference; the
// file is removed by an explicit delete through the file API or the Files page, and a stored reference
// to a file that was explicitly deleted reads as `gone` (`prelude.files`' contract for any handle to a
// removed file).

@"A lookup that found a value."
data found(@"The stored value." value: unknown)

@"A lookup that found nothing — distinct from a stored `null`."
data absent(@"The key that had no entry, as the environment resolved it (workspace prefixes applied)." key: string)

// One lookup's outcome. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type lookup = found | absent

@"Read the value at @key@, resolved against the surrounding workspace: `found(value)` or
`absent(key)` — a stored `null` is `found(null)`, so presence and value never blur. Pin an expected
shape with `json.validate[T]` on the found value."
request get(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string) -> lookup

@"Read @key@ as a string, resolved against the surrounding workspace, falling back to @fallback@ when the
key is ABSENT or holds a NON-string value — `get` folded to a plain string, the found/absent + string-shape
match a text cell (a note pad, a digest, a soul) otherwise respells. A stored `null`, a number or a record
all read as @fallback@, so a reader never branches on shape; reach for a bare `get` when a missing entry
must be told apart from a wrong-typed one."
agent get_text(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string, @"The value when the key is absent or not a string." fallback: string) -> string with get {
  match (get(key = key)) {
    case found(value => value) -> {
      match (value) {
        case string(string_value) -> string_value
        case _ -> fallback
      }
    }
    case absent(key => _) -> fallback
  }
}

@"Read @key@ as an integer, resolved against the surrounding workspace, falling back to @fallback@ when the
key is ABSENT or holds a NON-integer value — the integer twin of `get_text` (a monotonic counter, a stored
threshold). A number with a fractional part is NOT an integer and reads as @fallback@, as does a `null`, a
string or a record; reach for a bare `get` when a missing entry must be told apart from a wrong-typed one."
agent get_integer(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string, @"The value when the key is absent or not an integer." fallback: integer) -> integer with get {
  match (get(key = key)) {
    case found(value => value) -> {
      match (value) {
        case integer(integer_value) -> integer_value
        case _ -> fallback
      }
    }
    case absent(key => _) -> fallback
  }
}

@"Write @value@ at @key@, resolved against the surrounding workspace, creating or replacing the
entry (last write wins). Any value fits: a document, a data value, a `file`, a secret (sealed at
rest). A stored `file` joins the project's file library — it becomes a project file (visible on the
Files page) that outlives the writing run. Overwriting the entry does NOT free the file it
replaced; a file is removed only by an explicit delete through the file API or the Files page."
request set(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string, @"The value to store." value: unknown) -> null

@"Delete the entry at @key@, resolved against the surrounding workspace; deleting a missing key is
a no-op. Deleting an entry that held a `file` does NOT free the file — it stays in the project's
file library; remove it with an explicit delete through the file API or the Files page."
request delete(@"The path-like key, relative to the current workspace." key: string) -> null

@"A `list` entry: a key holding a value, directly under the listed place."
data leaf(@"The key, relative to the listed place." key: string)

@"A `list` entry: a path segment with more entries below it — descend by listing @path@ deeper."
data branch(@"The segment name, relative to the listed place." name: string)

// One listing's element. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type entry = leaf | branch

@"List what sits DIRECTLY under @path@ in the current workspace: a `leaf` per value-holding key and
a `branch` per segment with entries below it (sorted; a name that is both leaf and branch yields
both). The default @path@ of \"\" lists the workspace itself; a subdirectory path lists below it —
the path parameter is how a prefix travels for listings, since a listing has no key to carry it."
request list(@"The subdirectory to list, relative to the current workspace; \"\" is the workspace itself." path: string ?= "") -> array[entry]

// ── path segments: the one spelling a name in a key may take ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// A key is path-like and a workspace is a prefix of it, so a NAME that lands in either is structural: a
// worker called "a/b" is two directories, one called ".." climbs out of the workspace its tools were
// meant to be confined to, and one called "" collapses a prefix into a stray separator. The geometry the
// store's whole design rests on — an agent dispatched inside a workspace lives there BY CONSTRUCTION —
// is undone by a single unchecked string, and the strings most likely to reach here are the ones a MODEL
// chose (a worker it named, a metric series it invented).
//
// So the charset lives HERE, once, instead of being re-derived per app. The two halves are deliberately
// different shapes, because they answer to different owners: `safe_segment` is TOTAL and answers `null`
// for the app to turn into a corrective sentence (a model's bad name is an ordinary event, not a bug),
// while `scope` PANICS on a malformed path (a program that opened a workspace on unvalidated input has a
// defect, and a defect is the runtime's channel, not a value to route).

@"Canonicalise @name@ into a store path SEGMENT, or `null` when it cannot be one: trimmed, lower-cased,
and then required to be non-empty and spelled only in lowercase letters, digits, `-` and `_`. ONE
offending character rejects the whole name — this is not a sanitiser, and deliberately so: silently
stripping a `/` out of \"a/b\" would hand back \"ab\", a DIFFERENT name than the one the caller asked
for, and two distinct requests would quietly land in one place.

The charset is what a KEY can hold without meaning something else. No `/` (that is a new segment), so no
`.` and therefore no `..` (that would climb out of the workspace a facility exists to confine), no
whitespace, and no case distinction — a store key compares byte-wise, so \"Weight\" and \"weight\" would
be two series a reader sees as one.

Reach for it wherever a name a MODEL or a USER supplied becomes part of a key (a worker's directory, a
metric series, a topic): the answer is the name to store under, and `null` is the app's cue to write its
own corrective sentence back to whoever proposed it. The trimming and lower-casing are part of the
answer, not a precondition — pass the raw text and store what comes back, so the same name typed twice
with different spacing or case is the same cell."
agent safe_segment(@"The proposed name, exactly as it was supplied — untrimmed and in any case." name: string) -> string | null {
  let normalized = string.to_lower(value = string.trim(value = name))
  if (normalized == "" || !array.fold(target = string.split(value = normalized, separator = ""), initial = true, step = segment_character_step)) {
    null
  } else {
    normalized
  }
}

// One character of a candidate segment, folded: Katari has no character classes, so membership is a
// `contains` over the charset spelled out. The accumulator short-circuits nothing (a fold visits every
// element) — the strings are name-length, so the whole check is cheaper than the branch that would.
agent segment_character_step(@"Whether every earlier character was in the charset." accumulator: boolean, @"The one character being judged." value: string) -> boolean {
  accumulator && string.contains(value = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789-_", search = value)
}

// Whether a whole workspace PATH is well formed: one or more `/`-separated segments, each of them
// already canonical (`safe_segment` answers the segment itself). Comparing against `safe_segment`'s
// answer rather than re-checking the charset is what keeps ONE definition of a legal name: a path is
// legal exactly when every piece of it is a name `safe_segment` would have handed back unchanged.
agent is_safe_path(@"The candidate workspace path." path: string) -> boolean {
  array.fold(target = string.split(value = path, separator = "/"), initial = true, step = path_segment_step)
}

agent path_segment_step(@"Whether every earlier segment was canonical." accumulator: boolean, @"The one segment being judged." value: string) -> boolean {
  accumulator && safe_segment(name = value) == value
}

// @path@ back, or a PANIC when it is not a well-formed workspace path. A malformed path reaching `scope`
// is a DEFECT and not an outcome: the path a workspace opens is the program's own geometry, so a `..` or
// an empty segment in it means the program routed unvalidated input (a model's word) into its store
// layout without asking `safe_segment` first. Recovering from that is not a thing a caller can do —
// there is no correct directory to fall back to — and returning a value would let the run keep writing
// into whatever place the bad prefix produced, which is the one failure the geometry exists to prevent.
//
// A program cannot RAISE a panic (see the `prelude` header: `panic` is the runtime's own channel and has
// no declaration a program can perform), so the defect is spelled with the one total-to-partial door the
// language has — a zero divisor, which the runtime reports as a panic ("division by zero"). The message
// is therefore the runtime's own and says nothing about paths; this agent's NAME is what identifies the
// defect in the trace, and it exists for that reason as much as for the branch.
agent panic_on_unsafe_path(@"The candidate workspace path." path: string) -> string {
  if (is_safe_path(path = path)) {
    path
  } else {
    let _defect = 1 / 0
    path
  }
}

// ── scope: the workspace — the store's ambient working directory ─────────────────────────────────

@"Open the workspace at @path@ under the current one, for the extent of @continuation@ — the
`use store.scope(path = ...)` form, the store's `cd`. Four handlers catch the store operations and
RE-PERFORM them outward with the prefixed key (`get(key = \"index\")` under a scope at \"core\"
escalates as `get(key = \"core/index\")`; a `list` gets @path@ prepended to its own path
parameter), so nested scopes accumulate prefixes and the outermost bare perform is project-root
access — unhandled at the very top, the runtime machine-answers against the project's durable rows.
The handlers are PARALLEL on purpose: a scope is pure prefix arithmetic and must not serialize the
traffic passing through it (a turn's tool batch stays concurrent); ordering is `serialize`'s job,
never a workspace's. And a scope is a one-way descent: there is deliberately no `..`, so data
several workspaces share is NOT reached by path escape — it is reached by a REQUEST served ABOVE the
workspaces, whose clause opens the shared subtree's own scope in its body. That proxy is the
semantics: a request is what an inner install can never capture by accident. `share` / `shared`
below is that proxy, generalised once so it need not be respelt per shared cell.

@path@ is one or more `/`-separated SEGMENTS, each spelled as `safe_segment` requires (lowercase
letters, digits, `-`, `_`). A path that is not — an empty segment, a `..`, a stray space, a name a model
chose and nobody checked — PANICS here rather than opening a workspace somewhere else: the descent is
the program's own geometry, so a malformed one is a defect in the program and not an outcome a caller
could route. Validate a name that came from OUTSIDE the program with `safe_segment` first, and render
its `null` into whatever sentence the proposer should read."
agent scope[R, effect E](
  @"The workspace's path under the current one, e.g. \"core\" or \"workers/scribe\"; `/`-separated `safe_segment`s, and a malformed one panics." path: string,
  @"Runs with every store operation prefixed; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, get, set, delete, list},
) -> R with E | get | set | delete | list {
  // Clause parameter names are structural, so the `list` clause below MUST bind `path` — which
  // would shadow this agent's own `path`. The prefix is rebound once, before the handler — and the
  // rebinding is where the descent is checked, so no handler below can ever prefix with a bad one.
  let base = panic_on_unsafe_path(path = path)
  // PARALLEL on purpose: prefixing is stateless arithmetic, and a sequential handler here would
  // needlessly serialize a turn's concurrent tool batch through the workspace.
  use parallel handler {
    request get(key: string) {
      next get(key = f"${base}/${key}")
    }
    request set(key: string, value: unknown) {
      next set(key = f"${base}/${key}", value = value)
    }
    request delete(key: string) {
      next delete(key = f"${base}/${key}")
    }
    request list(path: string) {
      // An empty incoming path lists the workspace itself, so only the prefix travels outward.
      if (path == "") {
        next list(path = base)
      } else {
        next list(path = f"${base}/${path}")
      }
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── serialize: the serial domain — a FIFO whose extent is one dispatch ───────────────────────────
//
// Writes are last-write-wins, so a read-modify-write is NOT atomic on its own: two concurrently
// dispatched tools (a `parallel for` tool batch) that each read a record, edit it and write it
// back lose one edit. `serialize` opens a serial domain: `exclusive` runs a whole task as a
// CRITICAL SECTION inside the provider's own SEQUENTIAL handler, whose FIFO is the atomicity.

@"Run @task@ as a CRITICAL SECTION of the nearest enclosing `serialize`: the provider's SEQUENTIAL
handler calls it in its own body, so two exclusives of one domain can never interleave — the
handler's FIFO is the atomicity, even against a `parallel for` tool batch. The row is FIXED to the
store operations: a critical section reads, computes and writes, NOTHING ELSE — any other input is
computed BEFORE entering and closed over (as `modify` closes over its transform), so a section can
never block on a model, a network, or another domain. TWO GEOMETRY FACTS, both load-bearing: the
task runs AT THE SERIALIZE INSTALL SITE, not at this perform site — so a facility's subdirectory
prefix must be applied INSIDE the task body (`use store.scope(path = \"memory\")` as the task's
first move; a scope around the perform site does not travel with the task, but the install site's
own enclosing workspaces do apply). And an `exclusive` binds to the NEAREST enclosing `serialize`:
an inner domain shadows an outer one BY DESIGN — the nearest domain is the semantics, exactly as
the nearest workspace is. The task's answer crosses the handler as `unknown`; narrow it back with a
`match` (or `json.validate[T]`). A `serialize` (or `workspace`) in the program is the nearest domain;
with NONE installed above the perform, the RUNTIME serves it as the outermost domain — the task runs
at the PROJECT ROOT (project-root keys) in one durable, project-wide FIFO across every run. For a section
whose answer has a KNOWN shape, `exclusive_as[T]` below packages that narrowing."
request exclusive(
  @"The critical section; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list,
) -> unknown

@"The TYPED `exclusive`: run @task@ as a critical section exactly as `exclusive` does, then check the
answer against T's schema and hand it back as a T. Everything `exclusive` says still holds — the row is
FIXED to the store operations, the task runs AT THE SERIALIZE INSTALL SITE rather than at this perform
site, and it binds to the NEAREST enclosing `serialize`; the only difference is that the answer does not
cross back as `unknown`, so a caller stops respelling the same defensive `match` at every critical section.
T is read off @task@'s own declared result type, so it is normally inferred rather than written; it must be
a PUBLIC type, since a critical section's answer crosses `exclusive` as a public `unknown` and a secret
therefore never rides back out of one (read a secret from the store INSIDE the section instead). A
non-conforming answer throws `json.validation_error`, and that is NOT a condition to recover from: @task@
produced the value and its signature named the type, so a mismatch can only be the WRITER'S schema
mistake. Reach for the bare `exclusive` when the section's answer is genuinely shape-irregular."
agent exclusive_as[T extends unknown](
  @"The critical section; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> T with get | set | delete | list,
) -> T with exclusive | prelude.throw[json.validation_error] {
  // A task answering T is a subtype of the `-> unknown` task `exclusive` asks for (a result is
  // covariant), so it passes through untouched and only the ANSWER needs narrowing on the way back.
  // The `extends unknown` bound is what makes that widening legal: it pins T's privacy attribute to
  // public, which `exclusive`'s own public `unknown` answer already demands of anything crossing it.
  json.validate[T](value = exclusive(task = task))
}

@"Open a serial domain for the extent of @continuation@ — the bare `use store.serialize` form (no
arguments, no type arguments: the domain is WHERE it is installed). Serves `exclusive` with a
deliberately SEQUENTIAL handler: its FIFO applies critical sections one at a time, in arrival
order, which is the whole point (a `parallel handler` would forfeit the atomicity). THE INSTALL
DISCIPLINE: a desk's dispatch clause installs its workspace and its domain together —
`use store.scope(path = <its workspace>)` then `use store.serialize` — once per dispatch. The race
this closes is exactly one turn's parallel tool batch, so a turn-lifetime FIFO covers it, and the
turns themselves already serialize at the desk's own sequential handler; tasks re-perform their
store operations from THIS install site, so installing under the workspace is what routes a
critical section's keys into it. An `exclusive` performed below a nested install binds to the
NEAREST domain (inner shadows outer, by design); one performed with no install above it rides to
the run root, where the RUNTIME serves it as the project-wide root domain — project-root keys, every
run's sections in ONE durable FIFO — so install a domain when a narrower extent is the semantics."
agent serialize[R, effect E](
  @"Runs with `exclusive` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, exclusive},
) -> R with E | get | set | delete | list {
  // Deliberately NOT a `parallel handler`: the FIFO of a sequential handler is what makes an
  // exclusive a critical section — tasks run one at a time, in arrival order.
  use handler {
    request exclusive(task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list) {
      next task(value = null)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"Open a WORKSPACE at @path@ — the working directory AND its serial domain in ONE install: `scope`
composed with `serialize`, which is the DESK form (`use store.workspace(path = \"core\")` once per
dispatch clause). The two only ever separate inside a FACILITY, which opens bare `scope`s for its
subdirectory — its critical sections already serialize at the enclosing workspace's FIFO, so a
facility never opens a domain of its own. `exclusive` binds to the nearest enclosing domain as
always, so a nested workspace is a nested domain, exactly as it reads."
agent workspace[R, effect E](
  @"The workspace's path under the current one, e.g. \"core\" or \"workers/scribe\"." path: string,
  @"Runs with every store operation prefixed AND `exclusive` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, get, set, delete, list, exclusive},
) -> R with E | get | set | delete | list {
  use scope(path = path)
  use serialize
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"Read @key@ (a missing entry reads as `null`), apply @transform@, write the result back, and
return it — the one-key read-modify-write, atomic because it rides `exclusive`. @transform@ is
PURE: everything the new value depends on is computed before entering and closed over — the
critical-section discipline made a type. The stored value crosses as `unknown`; pin an expected
shape inside @transform@ with a `match` (or `json.validate[T]`), and remember a stored `null` and a
missing entry read the same here (use a bare `get` when that distinction matters)."
agent modify(
  @"The key, relative to the workspace of the enclosing serialize's install site." key: string,
  @"Old value in (`null` when the entry is missing), new value out; pure." transform: agent (value: unknown) -> unknown with pure,
) -> unknown with exclusive {
  agent update(value: null) -> unknown with get | set {
    let existing = match (get(key = key)) {
      case found(value => value) -> value
      case absent(key => _) -> null
    }
    let updated = transform(value = existing)
    set(key = key, value = updated)
    updated
  }
  exclusive(task = update)
}

// ── share: the shared place — ONE request for every cell above the workspaces ─────────────────────
//
// A desk opens its OWN workspace per dispatch, so knowledge several desks must share (a soul layer, a
// digest, a registry) sits ABOVE every one of them and is reachable from none — there is deliberately
// no `..`. The answer has always been a request served above the workspaces whose clause opens the
// shared subtree in its own body. That answer is right and it does not SCALE: a request per CELL means
// a `request` declaration, a handler clause and a re-spelt subdirectory scope for every cell worth
// sharing, a fixed cost per cell that grows with the app.
//
// `share` is that proxy with the cell factored OUT of it, on `exclusive`'s geometry exactly: the
// detail rides a CLOSURE rather than a name, so ONE request covers every shared cell. `shared(task)`
// sends the task UP to run where `share` was installed — that site's workspaces, that site's serial
// domain — and the task opens whichever subdirectory it wants as its first move. What a per-cell
// request bought is not lost: `shared` is still a request an inner workspace cannot capture, so the
// shared place stays exactly as unreachable-by-accident as it was.

@"Run @task@ AT THE NEAREST ENCLOSING `share`'s install site — that site's workspaces, that site's
serial domain — and hand back what it answered: the ONE request by which a desk reaches knowledge
living ABOVE its own workspace. The geometry is `exclusive`'s, and both halves are load-bearing. The
task is a CLOSURE that travels UP and runs THERE, so a workspace around this PERFORM does not travel
with it (the INSTALL site's own enclosing workspaces do apply) — which means a subdirectory of the
shared place is opened INSIDE the task body (`use store.scope(path = \"shared/soul\")` as its first
move), and that is exactly what lets one `share` serve every shared cell instead of one named request
per cell. And it binds to the NEAREST enclosing `share`: an inner shared place shadows an outer one BY
DESIGN, the same dynamic-scope rule the nearest workspace and the nearest serial domain follow. The
row is FIXED to the store operations plus `exclusive`: a task reads, computes and writes, and takes
ATOMICITY by wrapping a read-modify-write in `store.exclusive` INSIDE the task — which lands in the
shared place's own serial domain, since that is where the task is running. Nothing else is in the row,
so a shared access can never block on a model, a network or another domain. The answer crosses back as
`unknown`; narrow it with a `match` (or `json.validate[T]`), or reach for `shared_as[T]` below.
Unlike the store's four operations and `exclusive`, this one is NOT runtime-served, deliberately:
there is no project-root shared place, because a shared place is somewhere a program CHOSE and a
forgotten install must never quietly resolve to project-root keys. With no `share` above it a `shared`
rides to the run root as an ordinary unanswered request — the fate of any provider-backed request that
lost its provider — so the real guard is the effect row, which keeps `shared` visible in every
signature between the perform and the install."
request shared(
  @"What to do in the shared place; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list | exclusive,
) -> unknown

@"The TYPED `shared`: run @task@ in the shared place exactly as `shared` does, then check the answer
against T's schema and hand it back as a T — what `exclusive_as` is to `exclusive`. Everything
`shared` says still holds: the row is FIXED to the store operations plus `exclusive`, the task runs AT
THE `share` INSTALL SITE rather than at this perform site, and it binds to the NEAREST enclosing
`share`; the only difference is that the answer does not cross back as `unknown`, so a reader of a
shared cell stops respelling the same defensive `match` at every access — which matters more here than
anywhere, since a shared cell is read from several desks and each would otherwise narrow it again. T is
read off @task@'s own declared result type, so it is normally inferred rather than written; it must be
a PUBLIC type, since the answer crosses `shared` as a public `unknown` and a secret therefore never
rides back out of the shared place (read a secret from the store INSIDE the task instead). A
non-conforming answer throws `json.validation_error`, and that is NOT a condition to recover from:
@task@ produced the value and its signature named the type, so a mismatch can only be the WRITER'S
schema mistake. Reach for the bare `shared` when the answer is genuinely shape-irregular."
agent shared_as[T extends unknown](
  @"What to do in the shared place; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> T with get | set | delete | list | exclusive,
) -> T with shared | prelude.throw[json.validation_error] {
  // Exactly `exclusive_as`'s move: a task answering T is a subtype of the `-> unknown` task `shared`
  // asks for (a result is covariant), so it passes through untouched and only the ANSWER needs
  // narrowing. The `extends unknown` bound pins T's privacy attribute to public, which `shared`'s own
  // public `unknown` answer already demands of anything crossing it.
  json.validate[T](value = shared(task = task))
}

@"The TYPED shared CRITICAL SECTION: run @task@ in the shared place, as one `store.exclusive` of that
place's serial domain, and hand its answer back as a T — `shared_as` and `exclusive_as` in one call.

This exists because the two typed wrappers DO NOT COMPOSE, and the reason is structural rather than an
oversight. `shared_as[T]` reads T off a task that answers T; a task that wraps its body in
`store.exclusive` answers whatever `exclusive` answers, and `exclusive` is a REQUEST — its reply crosses
the handler boundary as `unknown`, which no inner `exclusive_as` can restore (the narrowing would have to
happen inside a task the outer wrapper cannot see the type of). So the read-modify-write of a shared cell
— the single most ordinary thing to do with one — fell out of both wrappers, and every site wrote a bare
`shared` around a bare `exclusive` plus a hand-written `json.validate`, each with its own paragraph
re-deriving why the helpers did not apply. This is that composition, spelled once.

Everything both wrappers say still holds. The task runs AT THE `share` INSTALL SITE (so a subdirectory of
the shared place is opened INSIDE the task body, `use store.scope(path = \"shared/soul\")` as its first
move), it binds to the NEAREST enclosing `share`, and inside it the section is atomic against every other
`exclusive` of that place's domain — which is the domain that matters here, since the shared place is
where the contention is. The row is FIXED to the four store operations: @task@ does not perform
`exclusive` itself (this agent wraps it), so it reads, computes and writes and nothing else.

T is read off @task@'s own declared result type and must be PUBLIC, for the same reason `shared_as`'s
must: the answer crosses two `unknown` request boundaries on the way out. A non-conforming answer throws
`json.validation_error` and is a WRITER's schema mistake, not a condition to recover from."
agent shared_exclusive_as[T extends unknown](
  @"The critical section, run in the shared place; store operations only — compute everything else before entering and close over it." task: agent (value: null) -> T with get | set | delete | list,
) -> T with shared | prelude.throw[json.validation_error] {
  // The one indirection the composition needs: `shared`'s task may perform `exclusive`, so the section
  // is wrapped in a task that does exactly that and nothing else. Both boundaries erase the answer to
  // `unknown`, so the single `validate` at the end is what restores it — narrowing once, at the outside,
  // where T is actually known.
  agent section(value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list | exclusive {
    exclusive(task = task)
  }
  json.validate[T](value = shared(task = section))
}

@"Open the SHARED PLACE for the extent of @continuation@ — the bare `use store.share` form (no
arguments, no type arguments: the shared place is WHERE it is installed, exactly as a serial domain
is). Serves `shared` by calling its task in the clause body, so a task runs HERE — under this site's
workspaces, over this site's serial domain — while the desks below go on opening their own workspaces
per dispatch and reaching nothing of each other's. THE INSTALL DISCIPLINE, in this order: the app's
workspace, then `share`, then the desks (`use store.workspace(path = \"assistant\")` then
`use store.share`). The order is a CONTRACT, not a habit: a task's row carries `exclusive` and this
provider re-performs it outward, so `share` must sit INSIDE a serial domain for a shared critical
section to land in one — `workspace` (scope AND serialize in one install) above it is the ordinary way
to satisfy that. The order is legible in the TYPE, not only in prose: install `share` above the domain
instead and its `exclusive` finds nothing to catch it, so `store.exclusive` surfaces in the enclosing
signature and those sections ride to the runtime's project-wide root FIFO — legal, and visibly a
different program. The handler is PARALLEL on purpose: sending a closure upward is not an ordering
decision, and a sequential clause would queue three desks' concurrent reads of the shared place behind
one another for nothing; ordering is `exclusive`'s job here as everywhere, taken INSIDE the task. A
second `share` below this one captures every `shared` under it — the ordinary dynamic-scope rule, and
the way a deliberately NARROWER shared place (a sub-team's own commons, shared among its desks and
invisible to the app's) is expressed. KNOW WHAT THE GENERALISATION COSTS: a request per cell is also a
CAPABILITY per cell — a desk holding `read_digest` and not `save_digest_entry` cannot write the digest
whatever it does — and one `shared` is one capability over the WHOLE shared place. Where a boundary
BETWEEN shared cells is the semantics, keep the named request for that cell; the two compose, since a
named proxy installed below this one can perform `shared` in its own clause."
agent share[R, effect E](
  @"Runs with `shared` served; its result is the provider's result." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, shared},
) -> R with E | get | set | delete | list | exclusive {
  // PARALLEL on purpose, and the opposite call from `serialize`: routing a closure to the shared place
  // decides WHERE, never WHEN. A sequential handler here would make the shared place a bottleneck for
  // every desk that merely READS it, while buying no atomicity the task's own `exclusive` does not
  // already buy.
  use parallel handler {
    request shared(task: agent (value: null) -> unknown with get | set | delete | list | exclusive) {
      // `next` re-performs from THIS install site, which is the whole mechanism: the task's store
      // operations and its `exclusive` escalate outward from here, through this site's workspaces and
      // into this site's serial domain — never through the caller's.
      next task(value = null)
    }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

// ── log: the bounded append-log — a named series that self-bounds and accounts for its overflow ────
//
// A store cell that grows forever is the shape every durable RECORD OF EVENTS starts as — a log of
// finished work, a metric's datapoints — and the shape none of them should stay. The bound is always the
// SAME three moves, written by hand at each site: append the new value, drop whatever overflows a cap, and
// keep a COUNT of what was dropped so a reader is TOLD the series was truncated rather than shown a quiet
// gap — a truncation a reader cannot see is worse than one it can. This factors that skeleton to a cell
// whose value is `{ items, dropped }`: the ordered series and the cumulative overflow in ONE record, so an
// append is one read-modify-write of one key and `log_read` / `log_dropped` are two projections of it.
//
// It ABSTRACTS the mechanism, it does not merge the callers: the SCHEMA is theirs (T is whatever the values
// are), the CAP is theirs (a `retention` value they pass), and the RENDER is theirs (the overflow is a
// number here; the sentence a reader sees is the app's). What the log owns is only append-bound-count. TWO
// policies for a full log, as a sum echoing `dedup`'s: `evict` keeps the newest and drops the oldest — an
// ever-growing series, and only an ORDERED log has a front to drop from, which is why the log is an array;
// `reject` refuses the newest and drops NOTHING — a capped set where a silent eviction would lose the one
// value that mattered (the entry evicted to make room could be the prohibition nobody chose to lift).
//
// NOT ATOMIC ON ITS OWN, by design: an append is a read-modify-write, so it takes its atomicity from an
// enclosing `store.exclusive` exactly as a hand-written one did — its row is `get | set` and nothing else,
// so it composes INSIDE a critical section that also does the caller's OWN writes (a completed task's move
// from the open record into the done log is ONE section: the append and the removal must not tear). The log
// deliberately does not open an `exclusive` of its own — that would split such a section in two, and a
// self-serializing append would be atomic against nothing the caller cares about.

@"A full log keeps the NEWEST @max@ values: a further append drops the OLDEST off the front and counts it
into the log's cumulative overflow — never silently forgotten. The EVICTING bound of an ever-growing series
(a done record, a metric's points); only an ordered log has a front to drop from, which is why the log is an
array and not a keyed record."
data evict(@"How many of the newest values to keep." max: integer)

@"A full log REFUSES a further append: the log is UNCHANGED, nothing is ever evicted, and `log_append`
answers `refused` so the caller learns the value was not stored. The bound for a set where the OLDEST value
is as load-bearing as the newest and a silent eviction would be the real loss (a capped registry whose
dropped entry could be the one that must not vanish)."
data reject(@"The count at and above which a further append is refused." max: integer)

// How a full log treats a further append: keep-newest-and-evict, or refuse-and-keep. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type retention = evict | reject

@"An append that LANDED: @kept@ values are now in the log, and @evicted@ of them fell off the front to make
room for this one — 0 under `reject`, and 0 under `evict` until the log first fills. The cumulative overflow
across the log's life is `log_dropped`, not this per-append number."
data accepted(@"How many values the log holds now." kept: integer, @"How many oldest values THIS append evicted." evicted: integer)

@"An append a `reject` log REFUSED because it was full: @kept@ values remain and the appended value was NOT
stored. A `reject` log answers this exactly when full; an `evict` log never answers it at all."
data refused(@"How many values the log holds; unchanged, because the append was refused." kept: integer)

// The outcome of one `log_append`. (Type synonyms take no docs.)
type log_result = accepted | refused

@"Append @value@ to the bounded log at @key@ under @retention@, and answer what the bound did — the ONE
read-modify-write behind every \"keep the newest N and say what fell off\" series. The cell holds
`{ items, dropped }`; this reads it, applies the policy, writes it back, and returns `accepted(kept,
evicted)` or (only under `reject`, only when full) `refused(kept)`. @retention@ is the caller's cap: `evict`
keeps the newest @max@ and counts the oldest overflow into `dropped`, `reject` refuses past @max@ and evicts
nothing. NOT ATOMIC on its own — an append is a read-modify-write, so call it INSIDE a `store.exclusive` (or
a `serialize` domain), where it composes with the caller's own writes in one critical section; it opens no
domain of its own. T is the value's type and rides the STORE JSON boundary, so it carries `extends unknown`
for the same reason `exclusive_as` does: the value is stored as a public `unknown`, and a secret narrowed to
public would leak — the WRITE side is where that bound is load-bearing (the read side, `log_read`, needs
none)."
agent log_append[T extends unknown](
  @"The log's key, relative to the current workspace." key: string,
  @"The value to append; the newest element of the series." value: T,
  @"The cap and its policy: `evict(max = ...)` or `reject(max = ...)`." retention: retention,
) -> log_result with get | set {
  // ONE read of the cell serves both the items and the overflow; a missing or malformed cell reads as an
  // empty log that has dropped nothing (the store's total-read rule, as `stored_done` follows it).
  let stored = match (get(key = key)) {
    case found(value => value) -> value
    case absent(key => _) -> null
  }
  let items = json.items(target = json.field(target = stored, key = "items"))
  let dropped = log_evicted_count(cell = stored)
  match (retention) {
    case evict(max => max) -> {
      // The bound is ARITHMETIC on the window, not an is-it-full branch, exactly as the hand-written sites
      // wrote it: an unfilled log slices from 0 and drops nothing, and `dropped` grows by an unconditional
      // `+ overflow` because adding zero is a no-op.
      let grown = array.append(target = items, value = value)
      let overflow = math.max(left = 0, right = array.length(target = grown) - max)
      let kept = array.slice(target = grown, start = overflow, end = array.length(target = grown))
      set(key = key, value = log_record(items = kept, dropped = dropped + overflow))
      accepted(kept = array.length(target = kept), evicted = overflow)
    }
    case reject(max => max) -> {
      // REFUSE at the cap: the log is left exactly as it was, so nothing an earlier append kept is ever at
      // risk from a later one — the whole point of the policy.
      if (array.length(target = items) >= max) {
        refused(kept = array.length(target = items))
      } else {
        let grown = array.append(target = items, value = value)
        set(key = key, value = log_record(items = grown, dropped = dropped))
        accepted(kept = array.length(target = grown), evicted = 0)
      }
    }
  }
}

@"Read the bounded log at @key@ as `array[T]`, oldest first — the values it currently HOLDS, the overflow
already dropped. A missing or malformed cell, and any element that does not fit T, read as an empty log
rather than a throw (the store's discard-the-unreadable rule, as `stored_series` follows it), so a reader
never branches on a hand-edited cell. T does NOT carry `extends unknown`, unlike `log_append`'s: a read only
NARROWS a public `unknown` to T, which never leaks — the bound is a write-side concern. Pass the same T the
matching `log_append` stored."
agent log_read[T](@"The log's key, relative to the current workspace." key: string) -> array[T] with get {
  let items = match (get(key = key)) {
    case found(value => value) -> json.field(target = value, key = "items")
    case absent(key => _) -> null
  }
  // `try_validate` is the TOTAL boundary read: a whole-array shape mismatch degrades to the empty log, so
  // one element an older schema wrote cannot blank a tool reply. A caller that needs per-element salvage
  // reads `log_read[unknown]` and validates each itself, as the digest render already does.
  match (json.try_validate[array[T]](value = items)) {
    case null -> []
    case validated -> validated
  }
}

@"How many values the log at @key@ has EVICTED over its whole life — the cumulative overflow an `evict` bound
counted, and 0 for a `reject` bound (which evicts nothing) or before any drop. The number a caller renders
into its own \"N older ones fell off\" notice: the count is the log's, the sentence is the app's, so exactly
one such line is derived per read and it can never accumulate in the stored series."
agent log_dropped(@"The log's key, relative to the current workspace." key: string) -> integer with get {
  log_evicted_count(cell = match (get(key = key)) {
    case found(value => value) -> value
    case absent(key => _) -> null
  })
}

// The cumulative-overflow field of a log cell, read TOTALLY: an absent cell, a cell with no `dropped` field,
// or a non-integer one all read as 0. The one place the `{ items, dropped }` shape's second half is decoded.
agent log_evicted_count(@"The whole log cell value, or `null` for an absent cell." cell: unknown) -> integer {
  match (json.field(target = cell, key = "dropped")) {
    case integer(count) -> count
    case _ -> 0
  }
}

// A log cell built from its two parts — the ordered series and the cumulative overflow — as the
// `{ items, dropped }` record every log operation reads back. Spelled once, so the shape lives in exactly
// one place.
agent log_record(@"The kept series, oldest first." items: array[unknown], @"The cumulative evicted count." dropped: integer) -> record[unknown] {
  record.set(target = record.set(target = record.empty(), key = "items", value = items), key = "dropped", value = dropped)
}
